# The prelude: every binary operator, as an ordinary function bound to its
# symbol, and its fixity. It is in scope everywhere, modules included.
#
# A fixity declaration gives operators a precedence (0, loosest, to 9) and an
# associativity: infixl (left), infixr (right) or infix (none). An operator
# without one is infixl 9. Application binds tighter than any operator, and
# prefix - and ! bind tighter than * but looser than ^.
#
# Operators are functions: (+) is the function, (+ 1) and (1 +) are sections,
# and a + b = ... or (+) = ... defines one. Overloading an existing operator
# with typed clauses is fine; its clauses are tried first, then these:
#     (Money a) + (Money b) = Money (a + b)
# Defining new operators is possible but not recommended: a named function is
# easier to read and to search for.
infixr 0 $
infixl 1 >>= >>
infixr 1 =<<
infixl 2 ||
infixl 3 &&
infixl 4 == != < > <= >= <$> <*>
infixr 5 :
infixl 6 + - ++
infixl 7 * / %
infixr 8 ^
infixr 9 \

($) = primApply
(||) = primOr
(&&) = primAnd
(==) = primEq
(!=) = primNe
(<) = primLt
(>) = primGt
(<=) = primLe
(>=) = primGe
(:) = primCons
(+) = primAdd
(-) = primSub
(++) = primAppend
(*) = primMul
(/) = primDiv
(%) = primMod
(^) = primPow
(\) = primCompose

# Functor, Applicative and Monad. IO and lists are built in; any other type
# joins by defining instances of map, >>= and return (as the option
# module does for Option and Result), which add to these methods everywhere.
#     map f m       m with f applied to what it holds or yields
#     return x      x in the monad the context decides (an annotation, a >>=,
#                   or IO when run)
#     mf <*> mx     the function mf holds applied to what mx holds
#     m >>= f       f applied to what m holds or yields, flattened
#     m >> k        m, then k: m >>= (_ -> k)
(<$>) = map
return = primReturn
(<*>) = primAp
(>>=) = primBind
m >> k = m >>= (_ -> k)
f =<< m = m >>= f
# Actions over lists: sequence runs each, yielding their results; mapM f xs
# runs f on each element; when and unless run an action on a condition.
sequence [] = return []
sequence actions = actions * action -> action
mapM f xs = sequence (map f xs)
when condition action = condition ? action >> return [] : return []
unless condition action = condition ? return [] : action >> return []
# Representations: to T x writes x as a T, from T x reads it back (to String,
# to JSON, to Chars; from String parses). They are methods: a program adds a
# type by defining to or from for it, as for map.
JSON = String
Chars = List Char
